      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2094621942964015564",
      "url": "https://x.com/xanderai/status/2094621942964015564",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/xanderai/status/2094621942964015564",
      "text": "The big world hypothesis = the world is infinitely complex, the approximations required for simulations will be catastrophic, therefore you must learn in the real world. The world is massively more complex than any mind/agent. \n\nEvery domain we want models to learn, self driving cars, drones, etc, requires many human engineers and domain experts to construct simulations and pour effort into the sim2real gap. If all the engineers went on vacation the models would stop learning. \n\nSynthetic data is wrong. It models a synthetic world, not the real world, and that matters. \n\nModels should learn from simulation but simulations that they make themselves. \n\nPriors = existing human knowledge. \nLearning = continual learning = constantly updating from experiences.  \nPriors and learning don\u2019t have to be at odds. You can take priors and use that as your basis for learning, but in practice they do end up being at odds. People who value priors have historically dismissed learning. \n\nSome abstractions arise only through communication. Ex: an elite athlete doesn\u2019t have a name for weird, niche things alone, but they develop terms for those things working with other athletes \n\nCatastrophic forgetting is totally solvable if you have the right algorithm:\n1) Step size optimization. Every weight has a different step size that\u2019s meta learned. \n2) Generate and test in feature space. Ex: continual backprop (generate = new random weights, test = the backprop)\n\nOur most ambitious project is to build a model that knows both the small details and the big picture. Uniform. Self maintaining. A single model. \n\n1T param model on 20watts in 5-10 years with the right algorithms. \n\nThere are infinitely many things so a single system can never learn all things.",
